The numbers agree with you. I measured the last 25 commits I wrote
that are on b/dev. Their median body is about 10 lines, and their
median diff is about 70 changed lines. Four of them change more than
250 lines. The four projects all sit well below that:

  project     median diff        median body
  ----------  -----------------  ----------------------------
  OpenBSD     10 lines, 1 file   0-1 lines; 29% are one line
  FreeBSD     17 lines, 1 file   4 lines; 15% have no body
  Linux       under 30 lines     5-7 lines
  PostgreSQL  32 lines, 2 files  66 words, about 6 lines
  lab.jj      about 70 lines     about 10 lines

For FreeBSD, OpenBSD and PostgreSQL, the figures come from recent
commits on main: 300-1500 for FreeBSD, 160 for OpenBSD, and 4231 for
PostgreSQL. The Linux figures come from 40 recent commits in
kernel/time. Treat them as orders of magnitude, not exact values.

You have already raised this on the list. You said "the commit
messages are wordy" and that any "what" should be bullet points [1].
You asked for a big commit to be broken "into logical changes" so
the series is "more evolutionary" [2]. You also asked for a refactor
to land "bug and all", with the fix in a commit of its own [3]. All
four projects say the same things.

> Thoroughly research them to learn their patterns. Review their
> documentation and explicit guidance. Read their commits - maybe some of
> the more well-regarded ones. Look for the best possible examples of
> commit style, both in terms of message and commit sizing and sequencing.

Here is each project in turn, then where they agree and where they
differ.

FreeBSD
=======

Guidance is the Committer's Guide, "Commit Log Messages" and "The
Big List of Rules" [4].

- Subject: "stay below 67 characters, and aim for about 50 or
  fewer". It may take a "component name and a colon" prefix, and
  should reuse the prefix used before for the same files. It is
  imperative ("when applied, this change will ..."), capitalized,
  with no period.
- Body: "Some trivial commits do not require a body." Otherwise
  "Focus on what and why, not how". The message must stand alone "in
  case these references are no longer available".
- Trailers: Reviewed by, MFC after, Sponsored by, PR, Fixes, and
  Differential Revision, which goes last.
- Sizing: "Do not mix style fixes with new functionality". Split a
  change "into multiple smaller patches that build on each other",
  and plan the split from the start, because splitting "is often
  difficult after the fact". For GitHub PRs, "Each logical change is
  a separate commit", and a PR should touch "roughly 10 or fewer
  files and fewer than 200 lines".

Examples:

  74d2a0b79ffd pf: Parse the GETSTATES state id as 64 bits

    The GETSTATES dump sends PF_ST_ID as a u64, but the request
    parser reads it as a u32 and rejects an id taken from the dump
    with EINVAL. Nothing in base sends PF_ST_ID. Parse and document
    it as a u64.

    Fixes: 2cef62886dc7 ("pf: convert state retrieval to netlink")

  These three sentences give the bug, its impact and the fix. The
  diff is 6 lines.

  dd51cef58311 sound: Whitespace cleanup

    In advance of additional sound(4) work, remove EOL whitespace
    and mixed tab/space.

  This is the labeled style-only commit that the rules ask for, and
  it lands before the functional work. It is 862 lines, yet the
  message is one sentence, because a mechanical change needs no more.

Linux
=====

Guidance is Documentation/process/submitting-patches.rst [5], plus
the tip tree's maintainer-tip.rst [6].

- "Solve only one problem per patch. If your description starts to
  get long, that's a sign that you probably need to split up your
  patch."
- "Describe your problem" first, then the user-visible impact, then
  the fix. The tip tree asks for "the context, the problem and the
  solution in separate paragraphs and this order".
- Imperative, "as if you are giving orders to the codebase". The
  subject is "subsystem: summary", at most 70-75 characters, and it
  is "a globally-unique identifier" within the series.
- "Changelogs written in abstract words are more precise and tend to
  be less confusing than those written in the form of novels."
- Each patch must build and run, for bisect. An API change and its
  first user go in separate patches. One mechanical change across
  many files is one patch. Post "only ... 15 or so at a time".
- Version notes go below the ---, where they are "automatically
  stripped off when applying the patch".

Examples:

  494af3ed7848 timers: Give a few structs and members proper names
    "Clean it up before we switch the wheel implementation over to
    the new scheme. No functional change."

  500462a9de65 timers: Switch to a non-cascading wheel

  Thomas Gleixner's timer wheel series. A rename lands first and
  says "No functional change". The redesign that follows has a
  message of about 90 lines. That length is justified: it is a
  design change with data, and it states its trade-off ("worst case
  inaccuracy of 12.5%"). It also states its scope: "This patch does
  not address the 'fast lookup' issue". So in Linux, message length
  scales with risk and subtlety, not with diff size.

  c21eaa72f02f posix-cpu-timers: Prevent freeing a timer which is
               queued on the expiry list

  A 40-line fix with a CPU0/CPU1 race table. It ends with the fix in
  one sentence: "Check whether the timer is queued on the expiry
  list or not instead."

OpenBSD
=======

OpenBSD writes almost nothing down about commit messages. FAQ 5 says
only to send an inline diff against -current to tech@ [7]. The style
is pure habit: one or two lines, often lowercase, with no
subject/body split, followed by who reviewed it.

Examples:

  8551051e7f3f (usr.bin/mg, +7/-8)

    remove unused findfname() argument
    ok tb@ florian@

  The diff went to tech@ with a one-line reason. Two developers
  replied "ok", and it was committed the next day.

  573f7b81039c (uvm_vnode.c, +3/-2)

    Revert rev. 1.134 of uvm_vnode.c because it introduces a
    reference race

  A 5-line kernel diff still gets two short paragraphs of why,
  because the reasoning is subtle. Even OpenBSD writes a body when
  the change needs one.

Sequencing is many tiny commits, each with its own ok. One recent
ocspcheck hardening landed as five commits of 5-20 lines each,
within 35 minutes.

PostgreSQL
==========

Guidance is the wiki: "Commit Message Guidance" (still marked as a
draft), "Submitting a Patch", and "Creating Clean Patches" [8].

- Subject under 64 characters, a complete imperative sentence.
- A narrative body, then machine-parsed trailers: Author,
  Reviewed-by, Reported-by, Discussion: https://postgr.es/m/<id>,
  and Backpatch-through.
- "Resist the temptation to build a giant patch all at once." "The
  fastest way to get your patch rejected is to make unrelated
  changes." "The new code you wrote is not the final product here;
  the patch diff is."

PostgreSQL is the odd one out: committers rewrite the message. In
99% of commits the git author is the committer, and the real author
is credited in an Author: trailer. A contributor's 0001..000N set is
either squashed or committed in order as separate commits. One
example is the pg_waldump tar series. It landed as four commits: a
move to common code, preparatory refactoring, the feature, and then
its consumer.

  94e1385ed285 Fix two edge cases in numeric log(base, val).

  Tom Lane, 33+/3- with tests. Three paragraphs give the bug, the
  new behavior, and the back-patch decision: "it's not clear that
  people would appreciate such a behavior change in stable
  branches, so fix master only."

> Not all of them will be the same, of course. Clearly identify where they
> are similar or agree, and where they differ.

Where all four agree
====================

1. One logical change per commit. Unrelated changes in one commit
   are the most common reason for rejection.
2. Preparation lands first and on its own. Renames, moves, style
   cleanup and new interfaces come first, marked "No functional
   change" where true. The behavior change comes after. Fixes to
   moved code are their own commits.
3. Every commit builds and works, so bisect and cherry-pick are
   safe.
4. Imperative subject with an area prefix: FreeBSD, Linux, modern
   OpenBSD, and about half of PostgreSQL. It runs about 50-75
   characters, and the subject alone tells a reader whether to care.
5. The body explains why: the problem and its impact, then the fix
   in a sentence. The diff carries the how.
6. A small or mechanical change gets a small message, often one
   line or none.
7. The message stands alone. Version notes go below the --- or in
   the cover letter. When you cite another commit, give its hash and
   subject.

Where they differ
=================

- Body length. OpenBSD is near zero, FreeBSD and PostgreSQL are a
  few lines, and Linux runs from a few lines to 90 for a design
  change. In all four, length tracks how subtle the change is, not
  how big the diff is.
- Who writes the message. PostgreSQL committers rewrite it and often
  squash the series. In the other three, the author's message
  lands.
- Trailers. Linux and PostgreSQL rely on them for credit,
  backports, and links to the discussion. FreeBSD uses them for
  review and MFC tracking. OpenBSD writes "ok tb@" in prose.
- Prefix. Linux's tip tree says "do not use file names" and wants
  subsys/component. FreeBSD wants whatever prefix the same files
  used before. Old-style OpenBSD logs drop the prefix entirely,
  because the commit mail already lists the directories.
- Series size. Linux caps a posting at about 15 patches, and
  FreeBSD PRs at about 200 lines. PostgreSQL says to send one patch
  normally, and to split only when the parts can be committed
  separately.

> I am looking for a thorough
> analysis of these commit styles, to inform future style guidelines for
> this repo.

Here is how I read it against what we do now.

Our format already matches the consensus. We use an area prefix, an
imperative subject of about 50 characters, a 72-column body, and we
explain why rather than what. The excess is in the content and the
cut of each commit:

- My bodies explain every decision in the diff, as one paragraph
  each. In the four projects, a body has one reason, and the
  per-decision detail goes in a code comment, or nowhere. That is
  your "wordy" point. When a commit needs more than one paragraph of
  why, it is usually two commits, which is Linux's own test.
- My commits bundle a new service with its config, credentials,
  checks and docs. The projects would land each of those as its own
  step.

If you want this turned into rules, I would propose these:

1. Body: say why in one short paragraph of 1-4 lines. No body at
   all for mechanical changes such as renames, moves, style fixes
   and typos. Any "what" goes in bullets, per [1].
2. Write more than one paragraph only for a subtle change, such as a
   race, a trade-off, or a rejected alternative a reviewer would
   otherwise suggest.
3. If the message needs a second reason, split the commit.
4. Land preparatory commits first, marked "No functional change"
   where true. Move or extract code as it is, then fix it in its own
   commit, per [3].
5. Aim for diffs under about 50 changed lines. Treat 200 as a reason
   to split, unless the change is mechanical.
6. Keep explanations of how the code works in code comments, not in
   the commit message.

